Third-party data: the IAPWS-95 coefficients

The file openflowsheet/_data/benchmarks/s05/iapws95_coefficients.json
(repository path benchmarks/s05/iapws95_coefficients.json), read by the
library-only property provider iapws95-v1, reproduces the coefficients and
constants of the IAPWS-95 formulation, IAPWS R6-95(2018). That release is
© 2018 International Association for the Properties of Water and Steam. Its
terms: "Publication in whole or in part is allowed in all countries provided
that attribution is given to the International Association for the
Properties of Water and Steam". Attribution is hereby given to the
International Association for the Properties of Water and Steam (IAPWS).

Please cite as: International Association for the Properties of Water and
Steam, IAPWS R6-95(2018), Revised Release on the IAPWS Formulation 1995 for
the Thermodynamic Properties of Ordinary Water Substance for General and
Scientific Use (2018).

Third-party data: the Wilson records and the points they are fitted to

The file openflowsheet/_data/benchmarks/s10/records.yaml (repository path
benchmarks/s10/records.yaml), read by the property providers wilson-ig-v1 and
wilson-ig-v2, holds parameters this project fitted and published values it
took, for methanol, ethanol and water:

- ideal-gas heat capacities and formation enthalpies from
  NASA TM-4513 (B.J. McBride, S. Gordon and M.A. Reno, October 1993), work of
  the US Government, public use permitted, read through Cantera 3.2.0;
- vapour-pressure parameters fitted to saturation pressures computed with
  CoolProp 8.0.0 (MIT licence; Copyright (c) 2012-2018 Ian H. Bell and other
  CoolProp developers) from the reference equations of state of methanol
  (deReuck-BOOK-1993), ethanol (Schroeder-JPCRD-2014) and water
  (Wagner-JPCRD-2002, the IAPWS-95 formulation attributed above);
- Wilson parameters fitted to the vapour-liquid equilibrium data of
  K. Kurihara, M. Nakamichi and K. Kojima,
  J. Chem. Eng. Data 38 (1993) 446-449, Table II (© 1993 American Chemical
  Society);
- molar masses from the CIAAW 2021 conventional atomic weights (H 1.008,
  C 12.011, O 15.999) of IUPAC's Commission on Isotopic Abundances and Atomic
  Weights.

The file openflowsheet/_data/benchmarks/s10/vle/kurihara1993_binaries.csv
(repository path benchmarks/s10/vle/kurihara1993_binaries.csv) transcribes
the 49 measured points (T, x1, y1 at 101.3 kPa) of Table II of that paper.
